<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Panama's Role in the World</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> How the Canal Impacts Panama's Future</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Evaluate texts for bias. Reading: Understand the relationship between main points and examples in various texts.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can evaluate bias in texts about the Canal expansion, identifying whether the text emphasizes economic gains but downplays the impact on biodiversity (e.g., "The text mentions job creation, but omits the effects on mangrove ecosystems.") Can understand relationships between trade benefits and environmental concerns in Canal texts (e.g., "While the Canal boosts trade, it also affects local ecosystems.") Can identify examples that support or contradict the argument that the Canal's expansion has increased national revenue at the expense of water conservation (e.g., "Some reports praise trade efficiency, while others warn about water shortages in Lake Gatún.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a split image on the board: one side shows a busy Panama Canal with large ships (economic prosperity), and the other shows a dry section of Lake Gatún or an affected mangrove area (environmental concern). Students brainstorm words related to each image, linking back to the vocabulary introduced in Lesson 1 (e.g., 'traffic,' 'shipping,' 'trade,' 'ecosystems,' 'environmental concerns'). The teacher then introduces the central question for the lesson: "How can we read about the Canal's future and understand different perspectives?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes two contrasting headlines on the board, for example: "Canal Expansion: A Global Trade Triumph" and "Environmental Costs of Panama Canal Expansion Rise." The teacher models reading each headline aloud, emphasizing the long vowel /oʊ/ in "global" and "costs," and models how to identify words that suggest a positive or negative stance (e.g., 'triumph' vs. 'costs'). The teacher highlights how different words influence the reader's initial perception.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a text mentions 'significant investments' in the Canal, does that sound like a positive or negative development?" (Expected answer: Positive.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does it mean if an 'environmental group raises concerns' about the Canal's operation?" (Expected answer: They are worried about negative impacts on nature.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 0-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An opinion piece excerpt from a Panamanian online news portal, moderately formal register, focusing on the Canal's economic and environmental implications. The text explicitly uses target vocabulary (e.g., 'global relationships,' 'efficiency,' 'investments,' 'environmental groups,' 'ecosystems,' 'impact,' 'raise concerns') and grammatical structures (e.g., "The Canal has been managed by the Panama Canal Authority," "If Panama had not built the Canal, global trade would have faced...", "The Canal has been improving global trade, but it has also been causing environmental concerns."). The text subtly presents two sides of the impact of the Canal's expansion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpretive Communication: Reading and evaluating informational texts about the Panama Canal's impact on its future, identifying main points, supporting details, and potential biases.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions (Whole Class):</b> Students read the text silently for 2 minutes. Then, the teacher asks: "What is the main topic of this article?" "Does the article primarily focus on the Canal's economic benefits or its environmental challenges, or both?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Individual then Pair Check):</b> Students read the text again and answer 5-7 True/False statements related to the main ideas and explicit details in the text. For example: "The Panama Canal has been operating since the early 20th century." "The expansion has led to no environmental concerns." Students then compare answers with a partner.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 8-17 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading for specific grammatical structures (passive voice, second/third conditionals, present perfect continuous) and identifying target vocabulary in context. Practicing evaluating how main points are supported by examples and recognizing different perspectives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Grammar & Vocab Spotting (Individual):</b> Students re-read the provided text. They underline all instances of: a) passive voice, b) second and third conditionals, c) present perfect continuous. They also circle five new or target vocabulary words (e.g., 'efficiency,' 'investments,' 'environmental groups,' 'impactful,' 'congested') and write a synonym or brief definition based on context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Sentence Completion / Gap-fill (Pairs):</b> The teacher provides a handout with 5-7 sentences adapted from the text, with gaps. Students work in pairs to fill in the blanks using either a target vocabulary word or the correct form of the given verb to complete a passive voice, conditional, or present perfect continuous structure. For example: "The Canal _______ (manage) by the ACP since 1999." "If the government ______ (not invest) in infrastructure, shipping ______ (face) significant delays." "The expansion project ______ (complete) in 2016."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Bias Scavenger Hunt (Pairs):</b> Students, in their pairs, re-read the text with a focus on identifying specific phrases or sentences that either emphasize economic gains OR highlight environmental concerns. They create two columns: "Economic Focus" and "Environmental Focus," listing direct quotes. For example, under "Economic Focus," they might list: "increased national revenue," "boost trade efficiency"; under "Environmental Focus," they might list: "effects on mangrove ecosystems," "warn about water shortages." This activity directly targets evaluating bias and understanding relationships between main points and examples.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 18-30 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task:</b> "Analyzing Contrasting Perspectives"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives two short, distinct excerpts (approx. 150 words each) from different sources (e.g., a pro-business editorial vs. an environmental NGO report) about the Panama Canal's future. One excerpt will focus on economic benefits and global influence, while the other will highlight environmental impacts and long-term sustainability challenges. Both texts will contain the target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Groups will read both texts critically. Their task is to identify the main argument and specific examples used by each text. They then discuss and create a T-chart or Venn diagram to visually represent:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The main points presented by each perspective.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Specific examples or evidence used to support those points.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Any evident bias (e.g., emphasizing economic gains but downplaying biodiversity impact, or vice-versa).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Connections between trade benefits and environmental concerns mentioned.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Finally, each group prepares a brief (2-3 minute) oral summary of their findings to the class, explaining how the texts present different views and how they identified bias, using target vocabulary (e.g., 'impactful,' 'investments,' 'ecosystems,' 'raise concerns') and grammatical structures (passive voice, conditionals, present perfect continuous) where appropriate. For example: "While the Canal has been improving global trade, environmental groups have been raising concerns about water conservation. If these concerns had not been addressed, local ecosystems would have faced severe consequences."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 31-40 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during group work and oral summaries, using a checklist to note students' ability to evaluate bias, identify main points and examples, and use target language. The submitted T-chart/Venn diagram and written summary will also be assessed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their completed T-chart/Venn diagram from the "Analyzing Contrasting Perspectives" task. Additionally, each student writes a short (5-7 sentences) individual paragraph reflecting on the main points and identified biases in one of the texts they analyzed, using at least three target vocabulary words and one instance of passive voice or a conditional sentence.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying main points and supporting examples from the texts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to articulate potential biases within the texts (e.g., "The text emphasizes economic gains but downplays the impact on biodiversity.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'impactful,' 'environmental concerns,' 'investments,' 'efficiency').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of passive voice, second/third conditionals, or present perfect continuous in their explanations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and organization of their written summary/paragraph.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"On a scale of 1-5, how confident do you feel identifying bias in a text about the Canal's impact? (1 = Not confident, 5 = Very confident)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new thing you learned today about how the Panama Canal has been impacting different aspects of Panama's future?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 41-47 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has today's lesson changed your perspective on how news or reports about the Panama Canal might be presented?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategy did you find most helpful for understanding the relationship between the Canal's economic benefits and environmental concerns?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The activity on bias identification was highly engaging. Students were able to grasp the nuances of different perspectives presented in the texts. The grammar focus on conditionals and present perfect continuous was well integrated into the reading analysis. Some students still struggled to differentiate subtle biases, requiring more explicit examples. I will prepare additional short, clearly biased statements for quick identification practice in future lessons. Pronunciation of multisyllabic words like 'environmental' and 'impactful' also needs continued reinforcement.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the knowledge gained from analyzing different perspectives and identifying bias to discuss and debate the long-term impact of the Canal's expansion, drawing on the vocabulary and grammatical structures practiced today. They will verbally express hypothetical ideas and discuss the Canal's impact respectfully.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 48-50 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>